03:28.378 --> 03:31.106
approval, and so that's what we are working to do today.

03:32.198 --> 03:36.406
Just going over entitlement funds, so the CDBG

03:36.936 --> 03:40.967
is available for targeted households that

03:41.467 --> 03:44.629
are below 80%. We get those funds from HUD.

03:45.180 --> 03:49.050
And these are the limits per household,

03:49.550 --> 03:52.360
and those are updated annually as well.

03:53.950 --> 03:57.890
Um, so the big item here that we're here to review— so ultimately

03:58.390 --> 04:01.930
we would love, um, council approval

04:02.430 --> 04:06.550
on these funding buckets that we have here. So we've allocated,

04:07.050 --> 04:10.130
um, as you see, we have the guaranteed, the, um,

04:10.630 --> 04:13.850
automatic 15% and 20% for public service and administration.

04:14.350 --> 04:18.089
Those are caps that are set by HUD. Um, we We have allocated

04:18.589 --> 04:22.809
$200,000 to housing preservation and/or development.

04:23.309 --> 04:26.809
That can be fairly flexible. Uh, we don't have to, you know,

04:27.309 --> 04:30.909
we're not zoning in on projects specific right now. We're just simply

04:31.409 --> 04:34.629
wanting to allocate funds to these buckets that you

04:35.129 --> 04:38.949
see here. Um, this is

04:39.449 --> 04:42.689
just a little bit of a breakdown for how we are looking to potentially utilize

04:43.189 --> 04:46.249
funds. Again, that's not anything that's been solidified right now.

04:46.749 --> 04:51.070
Um, we have our administration and public services.

04:51.570 --> 04:55.470
We already have our utility assistance that's been going and it's going great.

04:55.750 --> 04:58.950
We do have housing preservation and development, nothing that's

04:59.450 --> 05:03.270
been spelled out right now, but we have had some conversations around

05:03.770 --> 05:07.750
maybe down payment assistance or home revitalization.

05:08.270 --> 05:12.510
Again, nothing that's been carved out specifically yet.

05:13.010 --> 05:16.890
And then as far as public facilities, And neighborhood improvements,

05:17.340 --> 05:20.540
we've talked with departments about ADA and sidewalk upgrades,

05:21.040 --> 05:25.180
maybe some senior improvements. We did have a public comment regarding school

05:25.680 --> 05:29.440
community resource center, and so we're working to learn

05:29.940 --> 05:34.920
more about that and any other eligible activities as well.

05:36.060 --> 05:40.400
And so this fulfills our public hearing we've

05:40.900 --> 05:44.820
had open for discussion. So now we are just looking to

05:45.092 --> 05:48.914
Adopt and submit to HUD, um, the items

05:49.414 --> 05:53.270
that you've seen previously. And that is

05:53.500 --> 05:56.948
all I have. So,

05:57.013 --> 06:00.566
Kira, just for a clarification, that first slide— it's okay,

06:00.582 --> 06:03.676
it's gone now. Um, you can pull

06:04.176 --> 06:06.976
it back up. The— in the past, we have paid,

06:07.395 --> 06:10.930
um, MAG to do that, that 20% or that

06:10.963 --> 06:14.129
$39,000 roughly, and we're now currently keeping that

06:14.226 --> 06:16.154
in-house and you're managing that, correct?

06:17.922 --> 06:21.972
We are working through some transition, and this is a great— actually great to

06:22.085 --> 06:25.814
bring up. So we're— while it is coming in-house,

06:26.541 --> 06:29.674
we have substantial items that we've needed to

06:30.174 --> 06:33.456
clean up with HUD that's taken— so we're kind of in the middle of,

06:33.472 --> 06:37.312
you know, going into new project year but also cleaning up and becoming compliant

06:37.812 --> 06:41.326
with a lot of items in previous years. And so Elizabeth

06:41.359 --> 06:45.270
has come in to help with some of that heavy lift that I've I'm

06:45.770 --> 06:49.090
just not equipped to dig into with the amount of stuff that we've had kicked

06:49.590 --> 06:52.930
back. Um, right now she is— we're

06:53.430 --> 06:56.850
contracting with Elizabeth for our CDBG services out of our administrative

06:57.350 --> 07:00.990
fund. If we wanted to keep that ongoing, um, as you've

07:01.490 --> 07:04.870
seen, we have that 5-year in 2028 where we should kind

07:05.370 --> 07:08.620
of maybe be looking at what we want to do with the new 5-year plan.

07:08.830 --> 07:13.409
Does that include Elizabeth, and is that fully in-house? My recommendation

07:13.909 --> 07:16.873
right now would be to keep Elizabeth on board while we clean up and make

07:17.373 --> 07:21.572
sure that we just get a good clean-running program and getting, you know, good graces

07:21.604 --> 07:24.571
with HUD. So, so of the 20%,

07:25.196 --> 07:28.195
how— is it 50/50 that we're giving Elizabeth, or is it, uh,

07:29.526 --> 07:32.942
like, is all that 20%? Like, I'm just trying to help the,

07:33.295 --> 07:35.957
the council understand because I know this has been a conversation in the past.

07:38.310 --> 07:41.810
Elizabeth, feel free to step in. We're— we are right. We haven't executed

07:42.310 --> 07:45.490
a new contract. We just had a lot of moving parts.

07:45.990 --> 07:49.030
We haven't really had the discussion yet. And so this is a great time to

07:49.530 --> 07:53.050
have the discussion. But Elizabeth, maybe you can speak to your

07:53.550 --> 07:57.490
fees. Absolutely. So I think right now we're

07:57.990 --> 08:01.790
kind of in discussion about what that will look like over the next year or

08:02.290 --> 08:06.057
so. And I am really clear with the communities that

08:06.557 --> 08:09.760
I work with because I work all across the country that I am not a

08:09.808 --> 08:13.751
forever solution. I'm a solution to get through

08:14.473 --> 08:17.663
a period of time. So, you know, if that

08:18.163 --> 08:21.927
is over the next year or over the next 2 years through the rest

08:22.427 --> 08:26.062
of this consolidated plan cycle, then that's fine.

08:26.960 --> 08:31.032
You know, the management of these funds

08:31.224 --> 08:34.927
is tricky. There are a lot of moving parts and there

08:35.427 --> 08:39.410
are There are a lot of restrictions and requirements.

08:41.020 --> 08:44.220
So, you know, this would be something that if you were to manage

08:44.720 --> 08:48.260
this internally, you would want dedicated staff really

08:48.760 --> 08:53.060
working half-time on this activity.

08:53.410 --> 08:57.240
So right now there are some things we're in the middle of

08:57.740 --> 09:01.600
an environmental review monitoring because there were some

09:02.100 --> 09:07.360
challenges there. And HUD is actually— it's not just Equal Opportunity. HUD is Looking

09:07.860 --> 09:11.280
at every program that Meg had managed

09:11.780 --> 09:15.180
over the last number of years, including Lehi,

09:15.680 --> 09:19.280
Orem, and Utah County. So Eagle Mountain has it

09:19.780 --> 09:23.079
the easiest in the sense that they're only going back a couple of years because

09:23.579 --> 09:27.020
the program's new for Eagle Mountain. But some, they're going back

09:27.520 --> 09:30.600
to 2018. So there are some things right now that

09:31.100 --> 09:35.160
really need some concerted effort. and some specialization

09:35.273 --> 09:38.751
to kind of clean up, which I think is where, where I

09:38.913 --> 09:39.431
come in.

09:43.343 --> 09:45.849
Okay, I, uh, is there any questions from the council?

09:48.314 --> 09:51.545
I did, uh, I know the record will reflect this, but welcome

09:51.561 --> 09:55.198
to Council Member Wood too. He was online. Um, I didn't see

09:55.698 --> 09:59.020
him earlier. So, Mayor, this is

09:59.538 --> 10:03.017
Zach, if I could just ask a couple questions. Go ahead.

10:03.449 --> 10:07.281
It's more just for my own understanding of kind of who's

10:07.781 --> 10:12.426
