10:12.506 --> 10:15.841
employee of Eagle Mountain City, correct? Yes.

10:16.370 --> 10:20.185
Okay. And you're— are you kind of that half-time

10:20.685 --> 10:23.904
person right now? Kind of trying to be.

10:24.353 --> 10:28.248
And PB&amp;G is in no way, shape, or form fall

10:28.440 --> 10:31.069
under my purview or umbrella, obviously, for housing.

10:32.049 --> 10:35.004
There was just a scenario that I see needed tackling,

10:35.116 --> 10:37.926
and I decided to champion it.

10:38.087 --> 10:41.652
Okay, totally regretted it. So, here we are.

10:42.423 --> 10:45.635
Okay, got it. All right, so we need staff.

10:46.518 --> 10:49.392
And then was it, was it Elizabeth? Yes.

10:49.954 --> 10:53.230
And Elizabeth, could you maybe just share, just so,

10:53.246 --> 10:56.876
just so I kind of understand better, I'm imagining you've

10:57.376 --> 11:01.340
been brought to the table to help us run this program effectively,

11:01.370 --> 11:04.790
kind of train up Kiara. And Kiara is kind of seeing now this is

11:05.290 --> 11:08.410
a pretty big project. And you're training

11:08.910 --> 11:12.050
Kiara and kind of showing her the ropes. And, and can

11:12.550 --> 11:15.890
I ask, what is the fee that you charge and how is that

11:16.390 --> 11:19.950
paid? Is that paid out of these dollars that we're considering, or is it

11:20.450 --> 11:24.510
separate, a separate line item out of our city budget? It's budget neutral.

11:25.010 --> 11:28.430
It's actually paid out of CDBG dollars. So I

11:28.930 --> 11:33.047
charge an hourly rate. I bill in 15-minute increments. I do

11:33.111 --> 11:36.483
have a couple of folks on staff. So, you know,

11:37.109 --> 11:40.785
the tasks that need to be performed, it depends

11:40.801 --> 11:44.285
on what it is. So if we are out, as there had been

11:44.785 --> 11:48.797
some discussion, doing some surveying in town to

11:49.246 --> 11:53.437
define areas that might be eligible for this funding, that's probably

11:53.501 --> 11:57.558
not a great use for my time because I bill higher

11:57.719 --> 12:01.883
than some of my staff does. Okay. I'm, you know, I'm actually,

12:02.723 --> 12:06.128
I do this all over the country. I've worked from Virginia

12:06.628 --> 12:09.453
to Oregon, Ohio to Texas,

12:09.664 --> 12:12.324
so, and lots of states in between.

12:14.900 --> 12:18.374
You know, it's very— go ahead. Yeah, because you've come

12:18.390 --> 12:21.929
to a couple of our council meetings, I thought that maybe you lived in

12:22.429 --> 12:25.582
Utah. I live in Oregon. Okay.

12:26.030 --> 12:29.059
All right. So when you come out, we're basically

12:29.075 --> 12:32.311
paying for flights and travel and your time there.

12:33.240 --> 12:36.701
You're sharing some of that because I also consult with Utah

12:37.201 --> 12:40.402
County. Okay. And so they are actually a larger

12:40.902 --> 12:44.632
client. I have taken on cleaning up an even

12:45.132 --> 12:48.717
bigger mess that was created in the county and

12:49.217 --> 12:52.850
some challenges with HUD. Yeah. So, and I

12:53.350 --> 12:56.930
also do ARPA. I do rescue plan work with Utah County through

12:57.430 --> 13:01.010
the end of this year. So you get economy of scale. I'm already here.

13:01.510 --> 13:04.550
I see. And so you don't have to pay for every flight. You don't have

13:05.050 --> 13:09.430
to pay for every hotel night or every car rental. There's definitely economy

13:09.930 --> 13:13.310
of scale. Okay, thank you. And I believe that people

13:13.810 --> 13:17.230
should be paid what they're, you know, what they're for their time. I'm just more

13:17.730 --> 13:21.653
trying to educate myself. Yes. So what

13:21.701 --> 13:25.696
is that hourly fee? And then how long have we been

13:25.808 --> 13:29.145
working together? And do we have kind of like an annual— we're going through

13:29.161 --> 13:32.643
our budget. I know this is like neutral, but obviously we want to

13:33.143 --> 13:35.643
try and keep as much money in this program as we can.

13:36.461 --> 13:39.926
And, and, you know, if it makes sense for us to task

13:40.426 --> 13:43.536
somebody with a real half-time for this, then we,

13:43.761 --> 13:47.035
as a council, we probably need to consider Who would you train,

13:47.773 --> 13:51.177
or, you know, or things that we could lift off of Kiara's plate so

13:51.677 --> 13:55.143
that she can dedicate more time to this program? Yes.

13:55.496 --> 14:00.554
Okay. So my hourly rate is about $130

14:00.715 --> 14:04.151
an hour. That's fully loaded. And my staff

14:04.231 --> 14:08.470
is anywhere from 65

14:09.305 --> 14:11.608
to about 80. Okay,

14:14.520 --> 14:17.739
great. And how many hours would you say— I

14:18.239 --> 14:20.636
guess, how long have we been working together?

14:21.521 --> 14:25.144
We've been working together just a few months, and we've

14:25.644 --> 14:29.314
just kind of initially started to undertake kind

14:29.330 --> 14:33.496
of the unwinding and building process.

14:33.738 --> 14:37.612
So, you know, it's been A

14:38.112 --> 14:41.714
learning experience for me as I learn what's happened in the past.

14:41.859 --> 14:45.704
And so we're just kind of through that spot.

14:45.929 --> 14:49.663
So it's probably a— you would, would you say it'd be a little bit heavier

14:49.855 --> 14:53.252
here in the first year as we're just like trying to build it up?

14:53.412 --> 14:56.169
And with other cities that you've worked with,

14:56.906 --> 15:00.848
what is that time requirement on your part in your experience?

15:02.066 --> 15:05.760
So it really It varies. I mean, there isn't any

15:06.260 --> 15:08.190
